  "result": "Fixed: the quote command now prints **27.06**. Before the fix it printed 26.09.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` added the missing zero on the wrong side of the decimal part. With `\"12.5\"`, the fraction `\"5\"` was padded on the left with `rjust` and became `\"05\"`. So the price came out as 1205 cents ($12.05) instead of 1250 ($12.50). The math: 2 \u00d7 1205 = 2410, plus 8.25% tax (199) = 2609, which is the 26.09 it printed. Any price with one decimal place was affected; prices with zero or two decimals were not.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Now 2 \u00d7 1250 = 2500, plus tax (206) = 2706, which prints as 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_money.py`, which checks `to_cents` on `\"12\"`, `\"12.5\"`, `\"12.05\"`, `\"12.34\"` and `\"0.1\"`. All 7 tests pass. None of the existing tests covered `to_cents`, which is why the bug got through.",
